      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:aged-care-legislation-amendment-increasing-consumer-choice-act-2016-sch-1-sec-83",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "83 Transitional rules",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/aged-care-legislation-amendment-increasing-consumer-choice-act-2016#sch-1-sec-83"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "aged-care-legislation-amendment-increasing-consumer-choice-act-2016.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "aged-care-legislation-amendment-increasing-consumer-choice-act-2016.pdf, p.29",
      "values:originalText": "(1)\nThe Minister may, by legislative instrument, make rules prescribing\nmatters of a transitional nature (including prescribing any saving or\napplication provisions) relating to the amendments or repeals made by\nthis Act.\n(2)\nTo avoid doubt, the rules may not do the following:\n\n(a) create an offence or civil penalty;\n\n(b) provide powers of:\n\n(i) arrest or detention; or\n\n(ii) entry, search or seizure;\n\n(c) impose a tax;\n\n(d) set an amount to be appropriated from the Consolidated\nRevenue Fund under an appropriation in this Act;\n\n(e) directly amend the text of this Act.\n(3)\nThis Act (other than subitem (2)) does not limit the rules that may be\nmade for the purposes of subitem (1).\n\n\n\nAged Care Legislation Amendment (Increasing Consumer Choice) Act\nNo. 19, 2016\n\n[Minister’s second reading speech made in—\nHouse of Representatives on 11 February 2016\nSenate on 3 March 2016]\n\n(19/16)",
      "skos:definition": "(1)\nThe Minister may, by legislative instrument, make rules prescribing\nmatters of a transitional nature (including prescribing any saving or\napplication provisions) relating to the amendments or repeals made by\nthis Act.\n(2)\nTo avoid doubt, the rules may not do the following:\n\n(a) create an offence or civil penalty;\n\n(b) provide powers of:\n\n(i) arrest or detention; or\n\n(ii) entry, search or seizure;\n\n(c) impose a tax;\n\n(d) set an amount to be appropriated from the Consolidated\nRevenue Fund under an appropriation in this Act;\n\n(e) directly amend the text of this Act.\n(3)\nThis Act (other than subitem (2)) does not limit the rules that may be\nmade for the purposes of subitem (1).\n\n\n\nAged Care Legislation Amendment (Increasing Consumer Choice) Act\nNo. 19, 2016\n\n[Minister’s second reading speech made in—\nHouse of Representatives on 11 February 2016\nSenate on 3 March 2016]\n\n(19/16)",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:aged-care-legislation-amendment-increasing-consumer-choice-act-2016-sch-1-sec-83-norm"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "0edbbd2b48c9c1c5cbdfb31f704cb53bf68af27e315c223a7f4351244c0dea9a",
      "skos:note": "Minister may make rules for transitional matters"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/aged-care-legislation-amendment-increasing-consumer-choice-act-2016#sch-1-sec-83",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "83 Transitional rules",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 29,
      "values:originalText": "(1)\nThe Minister may, by legislative instrument, make rules prescribing\nmatters of a transitional nature (including prescribing any saving or\napplication provisions) relating to the amendments or repeals made by\nthis Act.\n(2)\nTo avoid doubt, the rules may not do the following:\n\n(a) create an offence or civil penalty;\n\n(b) provide powers of:\n\n(i) arrest or detention; or\n\n(ii) entry, search or seizure;\n\n(c) impose a tax;\n\n(d) set an amount to be appropriated from the Consolidated\nRevenue Fund under an appropriation in this Act;\n\n(e) directly amend the text of this Act.\n(3)\nThis Act (other than subitem (2)) does not limit the rules that may be\nmade for the purposes of subitem (1).\n\n\n\nAged Care Legislation Amendment (Increasing Consumer Choice) Act\nNo. 19, 2016\n\n[Minister’s second reading speech made in—\nHouse of Representatives on 11 February 2016\nSenate on 3 March 2016]\n\n(19/16)"
    },
